  "notes": "The highest-tempo vocal in the modern Memphis set — 152–176 straight (76–88 half-time), sitting at the top of the trap convention's window, and every other parameter here is a consequence of that. The kit runs hot and flat to match a shouted vocal: the loudest velocity tiers in this batch (accent 106–122, main 90–102), `velocityVar` 0.07, aggressive triplet hats at `fillDensity` 0.5 and the widest roll ramp here (46–96) with the largest jitter but almost no gaps (`gapProb` 0.06) — an unbroken roll rather than a stuttered one. The 808 is punchy rather than long-decay *because the tempo leaves less room*, which is the research's own reasoning: short `portamentoMs` 10–30 with fast glides. The harmony is the documented split — a single-line minor piano figure over a i drone with an occasional ♭II leaning tone on the rap singles, becoming a genuine 4-bar i–VI–III–VII pad progression on the melodic/R&B-leaning ones, so both are authored as families with the drone weighted heaviest. ⚠ `medium`, per the research: the fast-tempo characterisation, the piano-based beat description, the chart facts and the producer bench are sourced, **and the numeric tempo band itself is a reasoned inference**. ⚠ Nine short of the 500-harmony floor at 491. The single-chord family dropped from weight 5 to 2 and a fifth progression was added; the bII that carries this lane is untouched and still leads.",
